WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
Welcome to the Mole Concept.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.260
This recorded Chemistry class uses an AI generated Marin voice.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.540
Begin with what a chemical equation is allowed to change.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.020
In an ordinary chemical reaction, atoms are rearranged; the nuclei are not

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.240
converted into different elements.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.180
For a closed system, total mass is conserved to the accuracy of ordinary

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.100
chemical measurements.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.000
Gas escaping from an open vessel can make the measured vessel contents lighter

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.860
without violating conservation.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.660
The law of definite proportions describes the fixed elemental mass ratio of a

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.140
specified pure compound.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.320
Using rounded masses, water has hydrogen to oxygen mass ratio two to sixteen,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.060
or one to eight.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.080
Hydrogen peroxide is a different compound with a different ratio.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.920
The law of multiple proportions compares different compounds formed from the

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.380
same two elements: for a fixed mass of one element, the other masses occur in

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.620
small whole number ratios.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.280
Carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide illustrate oxygen masses sixteen and thirty

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.420
two for carbon mass twelve.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.020
Dalton's model helped explain these laws, but modern atoms have subatomic

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.240
structure and isotopes.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.020
Distinguish a useful historical model from its later corrections.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.080
A mole counts specified entities, just as a dozen counts twelve objects.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.985
The Avogadro constant is exactly six point zero two two one four zero seven

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:26.200
six

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.300
times ten to the twenty three per mole.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.725
For routine examples we will use six point zero two two times ten to the

00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:34.110
twenty

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:34.640
three.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.760
Always name the counted entity: atoms, molecules, ions or formula units.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.280
One mole of oxygen molecules contains two moles of oxygen atoms.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.780
One mole of sodium chloride solid describes formula units in a lattice, not a

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.820
collection of separate sodium chloride molecules.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.800
Molar mass connects mass with amount: divide mass by molar mass to obtain

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:57.160
moles.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.120
Using hydrogen one and oxygen sixteen grams per mole, water has molar mass

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:03.180
eighteen grams per mole.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.962
Nine grams of water is therefore half a mole of molecules, or about three

00:02:07.962 --> 00:02:08.240
point

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.280
zero one one times ten to the twenty three molecules.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.220
That same sample contains one mole of hydrogen atoms and half a mole of oxygen

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:16.600
atoms.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.820
A mole has a fixed entity count, but its mass depends on the substance.

00:02:21.288 --> 00:02:24.868
Read every subscript before calculating formula mass.

00:02:25.788 --> 00:02:28.208
Parentheses multiply the whole enclosed group.

00:02:29.068 --> 00:02:34.668
Calcium hydroxide contains one calcium, two oxygen and two hydrogen atoms per

00:02:34.668 --> 00:02:35.348
formula unit.

00:02:36.048 --> 00:02:41.064
Using calcium forty, oxygen sixteen and hydrogen one, its formula mass is

00:02:41.064 --> 00:02:41.408
forty

00:02:41.408 --> 00:02:44.368
plus twice seventeen, or seventy four.

00:02:44.368 --> 00:02:48.628
Its molar mass is seventy four grams per mole under those rounded assumptions.

00:02:49.278 --> 00:02:54.060
Thus seven point four grams corresponds to zero point one mole of formula

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:54.388
units

00:02:54.388 --> 00:02:57.148
and zero point two mole of hydroxide groups.

00:02:58.028 --> 00:03:01.768
Counting these groups is not a claim that every solid sample is already

00:03:01.768 --> 00:03:03.128
dissolved into free ions.

00:03:04.068 --> 00:03:07.888
Percentage composition is the elemental mass contribution divided by total

00:03:07.888 --> 00:03:10.968
formula mass, multiplied by one hundred.

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:14.843
For water, oxygen contributes sixteen out of eighteen, about eighty eight

00:03:14.843 --> 00:03:15.108
point

00:03:15.108 --> 00:03:16.448
nine percent by mass.

00:03:17.028 --> 00:03:22.588
A relative molecular mass is dimensionless; the mass of one molecule may be

00:03:22.588 --> 00:03:26.668
expressed in atomic mass units, while molar mass has units such as grams per

00:03:26.668 --> 00:03:26.788
mole.

00:03:27.488 --> 00:03:31.148
These quantities are related but should not be assigned interchangeable units.

00:03:31.988 --> 00:03:33.948
Use the atomic masses given in the question.

00:03:34.248 --> 00:03:38.168
An empirical formula gives the simplest whole number atom ratio.

00:03:38.828 --> 00:03:42.708
A molecular formula gives the actual atom counts in one molecule.

00:03:43.328 --> 00:03:47.828
Suppose a compound is forty point zero percent carbon, six point seven percent

00:03:47.828 --> 00:03:51.028
hydrogen and fifty three point three percent oxygen by mass.

00:03:52.048 --> 00:03:52.988
Assume one hundred grams.

00:03:53.988 --> 00:03:58.368
Divide by the rounded atomic molar masses twelve, one and sixteen: the amounts

00:03:58.368 --> 00:04:02.248
are approximately three point three three, six point seven and three point

00:04:02.248 --> 00:04:02.888
three three moles.

00:04:03.588 --> 00:04:07.668
Divide all by the smallest amount to obtain approximately one to two to one.

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:11.348
The empirical formula is C H two O.

00:04:11.348 --> 00:04:12.808
Its formula mass is thirty.

00:04:13.608 --> 00:04:16.764
If the measured molar mass is one hundred eighty grams per mole, the

00:04:16.764 --> 00:04:17.228
multiplier

00:04:17.228 --> 00:04:22.408
is six and the molecular formula is C six H twelve O six.

00:04:22.408 --> 00:04:25.388
Percentage rounding explains small deviations from ideal integers.

00:04:26.368 --> 00:04:30.788
Do not round one point five directly to two: multiply every ratio by two

00:04:30.788 --> 00:04:31.168
instead.

00:04:31.888 --> 00:04:36.088
Composition alone cannot distinguish all molecular formulas sharing the same

00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:39.828
simplest ratio, so additional molar mass information is essential.

00:04:40.848 --> 00:04:43.968
Check that the final formula reproduces the approximate percentages.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.724
A balanced equation is a recipe in amounts of substance, not generally in

00:04:48.724 --> 00:04:49.020
equal

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:49.420
masses.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.840
Nitrogen plus three hydrogen molecules produces two ammonia molecules.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.220
The same coefficients relate moles.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.600
Start with two moles of nitrogen and three moles of hydrogen.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.740
Divide each available amount by its reactant coefficient: nitrogen gives two,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.240
while hydrogen gives one.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.120
The smaller value identifies hydrogen as limiting.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.200
One mole of nitrogen reacts with three moles of hydrogen, forming two moles of

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.960
ammonia and leaving one mole of nitrogen.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.818
Using nitrogen fourteen and hydrogen one grams per mole, the initial masses

00:05:20.818 --> 00:05:20.980
are

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:23.980
fifty six grams of nitrogen and six grams of hydrogen.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:28.280
The products and remainder are thirty four grams of ammonia plus twenty eight

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.380
grams of nitrogen, again sixty two grams total.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.939
A limiting reactant is not necessarily the one with the smaller mass or

00:05:35.939 --> 00:05:36.320
smaller

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.040
raw mole amount.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.700
Convert to moles and compare against the balanced coefficients.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.472
This theoretical result assumes the stated reaction proceeds completely

00:05:45.472 --> 00:05:45.860
without

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:46.620
competing chemistry.

00:05:47.016 --> 00:05:51.076
Purity changes the amount of reactive material available before the reaction

00:05:51.076 --> 00:05:51.556
starts.

00:05:52.376 --> 00:05:55.876
Percentage yield compares the actual product obtained with the theoretical

00:05:55.876 --> 00:05:58.176
product predicted from that available material.

00:05:59.096 --> 00:06:00.416
Keep those two factors separate.

00:06:01.476 --> 00:06:05.536
Imagine ten grams of limestone containing eighty percent calcium carbonate by

00:06:05.536 --> 00:06:07.356
mass, with inert impurities.

00:06:08.136 --> 00:06:10.756
The reactive calcium carbonate mass is eight grams.

00:06:11.396 --> 00:06:15.976
On complete thermal decomposition, calcium carbonate gives calcium oxide and

00:06:15.976 --> 00:06:18.656
carbon dioxide in a one to one mole ratio.

00:06:19.536 --> 00:06:23.356
Using molar masses one hundred for calcium carbonate and forty four for carbon

00:06:23.356 --> 00:06:27.763
dioxide, eight grams is zero point zero eight mole and predicts three point

00:06:27.763 --> 00:06:29.376
five two grams of carbon dioxide.

00:06:29.836 --> 00:06:34.796
If the actual recovered carbon dioxide is two point eight one six grams, the

00:06:34.796 --> 00:06:36.236
percentage yield is eighty percent.

00:06:36.236 --> 00:06:40.656
The original twenty percent impurity must not be subtracted a second time.

00:06:40.876 --> 00:06:45.216
A measured yield above one hundred percent usually signals wet or impure

00:06:45.216 --> 00:06:49.596
product, a mistaken assumption or measurement error, rather than creation of

00:06:49.596 --> 00:06:50.216
extra matter.

00:06:51.216 --> 00:06:53.936
State whether impurities react before treating them as inert.

00:06:54.984 --> 00:06:59.424
For an ideal gas, pressure times volume equals amount times the gas constant

00:06:59.424 --> 00:07:00.744
times absolute temperature.

00:07:01.944 --> 00:07:05.675
Gas volume is therefore not a universal measure of moles unless temperature

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:05.824
and

00:07:05.824 --> 00:07:06.704
pressure are specified.

00:07:07.844 --> 00:07:12.170
At two hundred seventy three point one five kelvin and one atmosphere, one

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:12.404
mole

00:07:12.404 --> 00:07:17.084
of ideal gas occupies about twenty two point four one litres.

00:07:17.084 --> 00:07:21.731
At the same temperature and one bar, the value is about twenty two point seven

00:07:21.731 --> 00:07:22.554
one litres.

00:07:22.554 --> 00:07:25.004
Do not silently interchange these conventions.

00:07:26.124 --> 00:07:30.084
Equal gas volumes at equal temperature and pressure contain equal amounts in

00:07:30.084 --> 00:07:31.364
the ideal gas approximation.

00:07:32.504 --> 00:07:37.239
For solutions, molarity is solute moles divided by solution volume in litres.

00:07:37.239 --> 00:07:42.084
Zero point two zero mole in five hundred millilitres of solution gives zero

00:07:42.084 --> 00:07:44.264
point four zero mole per litre.

00:07:44.264 --> 00:07:48.584
During dilution without reaction or loss, solute amount stays constant, so

00:07:48.584 --> 00:07:52.704
initial concentration times initial volume equals final concentration times

00:07:52.704 --> 00:07:53.344
final volume.

00:07:54.524 --> 00:07:57.877
Taking fifty millilitres of this solution and diluting to two hundred

00:07:57.877 --> 00:08:01.544
millilitres gives zero point one zero mole per litre.

00:08:01.544 --> 00:08:04.784
Use final solution volume, not merely the volume of water added.

00:08:04.992 --> 00:08:10.152
A reliable mixed problem follows a chain of quantities with units attached.

00:08:11.032 --> 00:08:15.812
Suppose five grams of a sample contains eighty percent magnesium by mass and

00:08:15.812 --> 00:08:16.672
inert material.

00:08:17.692 --> 00:08:23.472
Excess acid reacts with magnesium to give magnesium ions and hydrogen gas, one

00:08:23.472 --> 00:08:25.612
mole of hydrogen per mole of magnesium.

00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:30.679
With magnesium molar mass twenty four grams per mole, the reactive mass is

00:08:30.679 --> 00:08:30.912
four

00:08:30.912 --> 00:08:33.472
grams and the amount is one sixth of a mole.

00:08:34.232 --> 00:08:38.792
At the stated ideal gas molar volume of twenty four litres per mole, complete

00:08:38.792 --> 00:08:40.832
reaction predicts four litres of hydrogen.

00:08:41.812 --> 00:08:45.832
If only seventy five percent of that theoretical gas is recovered, the

00:08:45.832 --> 00:08:48.972
collected volume is three litres at those same conditions.

00:08:50.432 --> 00:08:54.612
The acid is explicitly in excess; otherwise its amount would also need

00:08:54.612 --> 00:08:54.932
checking.

00:08:55.852 --> 00:09:00.592
Do not multiply by a percentage twice or apply a mass ratio directly as a mole

00:09:00.592 --> 00:09:00.992
ratio.

00:09:01.712 --> 00:09:06.092
Keep extra digits during calculation and round at the end according to the

00:09:06.092 --> 00:09:06.892
supplied measurements.

00:09:08.192 --> 00:09:11.732
Exact coefficients and entity definitions do not limit significant figures.

00:09:12.672 --> 00:09:16.352
Use the practice questions to check each conversion before attempting longer

00:09:16.352 --> 00:09:17.152
reaction chains.

00:09:17.712 --> 00:09:22.632
A hydrate calculation uses the mole ratio of water to the anhydrous salt, not

00:09:22.632 --> 00:09:24.212
the ratio of their measured masses.

00:09:25.052 --> 00:09:29.072
Suppose supplied analysis says a two and one half gram hydrated copper sulfate

00:09:29.072 --> 00:09:33.332
sample corresponds to one point six zero grams of anhydrous copper sulfate

00:09:33.332 --> 00:09:35.392
after complete loss of water.

00:09:35.932 --> 00:09:40.072
Assume only water is lost, no salt decomposes, and the sample has the stated

00:09:40.072 --> 00:09:40.932
pure composition.

00:09:40.932 --> 00:09:43.752
Water mass is zero point nine zero gram.

00:09:44.632 --> 00:09:48.312
Using rounded molar masses eighteen for water and one hundred sixty grams per

00:09:48.312 --> 00:09:52.452
mole for anhydrous copper sulfate gives zero point zero five zero mole water

00:09:52.452 --> 00:09:54.852
and zero point zero one zero mole salt.

00:09:55.812 --> 00:10:00.012
Their ratio is five, so the formula is copper sulfate with five waters of

00:10:00.012 --> 00:10:00.632
crystallization.

00:10:01.412 --> 00:10:04.092
The anhydrous formula unit remains the reference entity.

00:10:04.872 --> 00:10:08.492
Incomplete water removal or loss of other material would invalidate the

00:10:08.492 --> 00:10:08.952
interpretation.

00:10:09.502 --> 00:10:12.792
A near integer ratio should be judged against the supplied measurement

00:10:12.792 --> 00:10:16.192
precision rather than rounded aggressively from incompatible data.

00:10:17.172 --> 00:10:21.372
This is interpretation of provided analytical results, not an instruction to

00:10:21.372 --> 00:10:24.192
heat a substance without an appropriate supervised method.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.360
Two independent measurements can determine a binary mixture when the reaction

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:30.420
model is specified.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:35.280
Take a four point six four gram mixture containing only anhydrous sodium

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.800
carbonate and sodium hydrogen carbonate.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.510
Complete reaction with sufficient strong acid gives two point two zero grams

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:43.640
of

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:44.280
carbon dioxide.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.671
Using carbon dioxide molar mass forty four gives zero point zero five zero

00:10:49.671 --> 00:10:49.900
mole

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:50.280
gas.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.600
Each formula unit of either salt gives one carbon dioxide, so their mole

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:01.180
amounts a and b satisfy a plus b equals zero point zero five zero.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:06.443
The mass equation is one hundred six a plus eighty four b equals four point

00:11:06.443 --> 00:11:06.653
six

00:11:06.653 --> 00:11:07.160
four.

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:12.240
Substitution gives a equals zero point zero two zero and b equals zero point

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.100
zero three zero mole.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:17.920
The acid requirement is different: carbonate consumes two hydrogen ions per

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.980
formula unit, while hydrogen carbonate consumes one.

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:25.340
Total hydrogen ion consumption is therefore zero point zero seven zero mole.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:30.240
Do not confuse equal gas coefficients with equal acid coefficients.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:35.680
Check nonnegative amounts and reconstruct both total mass and gas yield before

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:40.140
accepting the solution; additional impurities or incomplete gas recovery

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.580
require a different model.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:46.080
A reaction chain requires both stoichiometric coefficients and the yield of

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:46.600
each stage.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:52.600
Consider the supplied abstract equations two A giving one B, followed by one B

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:54.280
giving three C.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.500
Start with zero point one zero zero mole A.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.043
At complete first stage conversion the theoretical B amount would be zero

00:12:01.043 --> 00:12:01.316
point

00:12:01.316 --> 00:12:02.900
zero five zero mole.

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:07.367
An eighty percent isolated first stage yield supplies zero point zero four

00:12:07.367 --> 00:12:07.608
zero

00:12:07.608 --> 00:12:09.520
mole B to the second stage.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:13.580
That B could theoretically give zero point one two zero mole C.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.512
A seventy five percent second stage yield therefore gives zero point zero nine

00:12:18.512 --> 00:12:19.280
zero mole C.

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:23.775
Relative to the original A, the full ideal chain could have produced zero

00:12:23.775 --> 00:12:24.020
point

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:28.340
one five zero mole C, so the overall yield is sixty percent.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:32.106
Multiplying zero point eight by zero point seven five gives the same

00:12:32.106 --> 00:12:32.660
fractional

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.940
result because the second yield is defined relative to the intermediate

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:36.880
actually supplied.

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:41.440
Do not add the percentages or apply both yields directly to the initial mole

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.540
amount while ignoring coefficient changes.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:48.120
Side reactions, intermediate purity and recycled material need additional

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.900
information before this simplified chain model can be used.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.860
Complete combustion products can reveal a hydrocarbon formula when the amount

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.700
of fuel is independently known.

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:03.935
Suppose zero point one zero zero mole of a pure hydrocarbon gives zero point

00:13:03.935 --> 00:13:08.860
three zero zero mole carbon dioxide and zero point four zero zero mole water.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:14.260
Each carbon dioxide contains one carbon atom, so each fuel molecule contains

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:15.060
three carbons.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:20.180
Each water contains two hydrogen atoms, giving eight hydrogens per fuel

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:20.560
molecule.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:26.200
The molecular formula is therefore C three H eight, not merely its carbon to

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:27.040
hydrogen empirical ratio.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:32.020
The balanced combustion equation consumes five oxygen molecules per fuel

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:35.900
molecule, producing three carbon dioxide and four water molecules.

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:41.160
For the stated fuel amount, oxygen demand is zero point five zero zero mole.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:46.020
Oxygen atoms in the products total one point zero zero mole of atoms,

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.820
equivalent to half that many moles of diatomic oxygen.

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.480
Keep atoms and molecules distinct.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:55.702
This inference assumes complete combustion and quantitative product

00:13:55.702 --> 00:13:56.260
accounting.

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:01.580
If the fuel contains oxygen or the starting fuel mole amount is unknown, the

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.820
same two product measurements alone require a different analysis.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.600
Physical states also matter when converting product moles into measured gas

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:09.980
volumes.

00:14:10.224 --> 00:14:13.984
Mass fraction, molarity and molality describe different denominators.

00:14:14.964 --> 00:14:18.964
Take a supplied twenty point zero percent by mass sodium hydroxide solution

00:14:18.964 --> 00:14:23.093
with density one point two zero grams per millilitre at the stated

00:14:23.093 --> 00:14:23.844
temperature.

00:14:24.604 --> 00:14:27.344
A one litre sample has total mass twelve hundred grams.

00:14:27.344 --> 00:14:32.004
Twenty percent of that is two hundred forty grams sodium hydroxide, or six

00:14:32.004 --> 00:14:35.484
point zero zero moles using molar mass forty.

00:14:35.484 --> 00:14:38.404
Dividing by one litre gives six point zero zero molar.

00:14:38.804 --> 00:14:43.417
The solvent mass is twelve hundred minus two hundred forty, or nine hundred

00:14:43.417 --> 00:14:44.024
sixty grams.

00:14:45.184 --> 00:14:49.504
Dividing six moles by zero point nine six zero kilogram solvent gives molality

00:14:49.504 --> 00:14:51.464
six point two five moles per kilogram.

00:14:52.044 --> 00:14:56.324
The two numerical concentrations are different because one denominator is

00:14:56.324 --> 00:14:58.424
solution volume and the other solvent mass.

00:14:59.764 --> 00:15:03.204
Density supplies the link between the given mass fraction and solution volume;

00:15:03.304 --> 00:15:07.704
it does not justify assuming that volumes of solute and solvent add exactly.

00:15:08.724 --> 00:15:11.024
Molarity depends on temperature through solution volume.

00:15:11.964 --> 00:15:15.364
For this conversion use the density corresponding to the same specified

00:15:15.364 --> 00:15:19.544
solution and temperature, and do not substitute solvent density for solution

00:15:19.544 --> 00:15:19.924
density.

00:15:20.328 --> 00:15:24.808
A back titration uses an excess reagent, then measures how much remains.

00:15:25.928 --> 00:15:30.508
Suppose a one point zero zero gram sample contains calcium carbonate and an

00:15:30.508 --> 00:15:32.528
impurity that does not consume acid.

00:15:33.228 --> 00:15:37.548
The supplied acid amount is forty point zero millilitres of zero point five

00:15:37.548 --> 00:15:39.428
zero zero molar hydrochloric acid.

00:15:40.068 --> 00:15:44.008
After the carbonate reaction, the remaining acid consumes ten point zero

00:15:44.008 --> 00:15:48.308
millilitres of zero point four zero zero molar sodium hydroxide in a

00:15:48.308 --> 00:15:50.108
quantitative one to one neutralization.

00:15:51.088 --> 00:15:54.328
Initial hydrogen ion amount is zero point zero two zero zero mole.

00:15:55.368 --> 00:15:59.408
Remaining amount is zero point zero zero four zero zero mole, so the sample

00:15:59.408 --> 00:16:02.148
consumed zero point zero one six zero mole.

00:16:03.068 --> 00:16:07.248
Calcium carbonate needs two hydrogen ions per formula unit, giving zero point

00:16:07.248 --> 00:16:09.248
zero zero eight zero zero mole carbonate.

00:16:09.828 --> 00:16:14.383
With supplied molar mass one hundred grams per mole, its mass is zero point

00:16:14.383 --> 00:16:18.168
eight zero zero gram and the sample purity is eighty percent.

00:16:18.168 --> 00:16:22.048
This inference needs complete carbonate reaction, the stated acid inert

00:16:22.048 --> 00:16:24.788
impurity and a valid residual acid endpoint.

00:16:25.788 --> 00:16:28.948
Other acid consuming substances would change what the assay measures.

00:16:29.184 --> 00:16:33.504
An oxide mixture can be solved from independent iron and oxygen balances.

00:16:34.564 --> 00:16:38.564
Consider a supplied two point three two gram mixture containing only iron two

00:16:38.564 --> 00:16:40.384
oxide and iron three oxide.

00:16:41.264 --> 00:16:45.084
Complete reduction gives one point six eight grams iron, with all original

00:16:45.084 --> 00:16:46.764
oxygen removed as water.

00:16:47.004 --> 00:16:51.104
Use atomic masses iron fifty six and oxygen sixteen.

00:16:51.104 --> 00:16:54.144
Iron amount is zero point zero three zero zero mole atoms.

00:16:54.144 --> 00:16:59.084
The removed oxygen mass is zero point six four gram, corresponding to zero

00:16:59.084 --> 00:17:01.824
point zero four zero zero mole oxygen atoms.

00:17:02.864 --> 00:17:06.004
Let a and b be moles of iron two oxide and iron three oxide.

00:17:06.824 --> 00:17:11.604
Iron balance gives a plus two b equal to zero point zero three zero zero.

00:17:11.604 --> 00:17:17.184
Oxygen balance gives a plus three b equal to zero point zero four zero zero.

00:17:17.184 --> 00:17:20.924
Subtraction gives b zero point zero one zero zero mole and then a the same

00:17:20.924 --> 00:17:21.204
amount.

00:17:21.724 --> 00:17:25.604
Their masses are zero point seven two zero and one point six zero grams.

00:17:26.644 --> 00:17:31.024
The atom balances also predict zero point zero four zero zero mole water if

00:17:31.024 --> 00:17:34.924
each removed oxygen forms one water molecule in the stipulated complete

00:17:34.924 --> 00:17:35.604
reduction model.

00:17:35.904 --> 00:17:40.424
An average atomic mass can give isotope number fractions under a stated two

00:17:40.424 --> 00:17:41.144
isotope model.

00:17:42.444 --> 00:17:46.334
Suppose chlorine has only mass thirty five and mass thirty seven isotopes,

00:17:46.334 --> 00:17:46.544
with

00:17:46.544 --> 00:17:48.904
supplied average mass thirty five point five.

00:17:49.484 --> 00:17:55.444
If f is the heavy isotope fraction, thirty five times one minus f plus thirty

00:17:55.444 --> 00:17:59.804
seven times f equals thirty five point five, giving f one quarter.

00:18:00.064 --> 00:18:02.464
The light isotope fraction is three quarters.

00:18:03.214 --> 00:18:06.813
For independent random pairing into diatomic chlorine, the light light

00:18:06.813 --> 00:18:07.224
fraction

00:18:07.224 --> 00:18:08.424
is nine sixteenths.

00:18:09.164 --> 00:18:13.164
The mixed pair fraction is six sixteenths, because light heavy and heavy light

00:18:13.164 --> 00:18:16.164
are two ways of obtaining the same mixed isotopologue.

00:18:17.124 --> 00:18:19.144
The heavy heavy fraction is one sixteenth.

00:18:19.844 --> 00:18:23.464
The molecular mass labels are seventy, seventy two and seventy four in this

00:18:23.464 --> 00:18:27.284
integer mass model, and their weighted average is seventy one.

00:18:27.284 --> 00:18:30.024
These are number fractions, not isotope mass fractions.

00:18:30.584 --> 00:18:34.744
Actual isotope masses differ from integer mass numbers, and random pairing is

00:18:34.744 --> 00:18:36.384
an explicit statistical assumption.

00:18:37.664 --> 00:18:41.264
Identify the counted entity before transferring an atomic abundance directly

00:18:41.264 --> 00:18:42.744
into a molecular peak ratio.

00:18:43.008 --> 00:18:47.528
A binary hydrocarbon mixture needs both a total amount balance and a carbon

00:18:47.528 --> 00:18:47.948
balance.

00:18:49.008 --> 00:18:53.308
Suppose zero point one zero zero mole of a gas mixture contains only methane

00:18:53.308 --> 00:18:54.088
and ethane.

00:18:54.868 --> 00:18:58.628
Complete combustion produces zero point one six zero mole carbon dioxide.

00:18:59.528 --> 00:19:01.848
Let a and b be the methane and ethane amounts.

00:19:02.448 --> 00:19:07.088
The total balance is a plus b equal to zero point one zero zero.

00:19:07.088 --> 00:19:11.712
Since methane contributes one carbon and ethane two, the carbon dioxide

00:19:11.712 --> 00:19:12.168
balance

00:19:12.168 --> 00:19:16.108
is a plus two b equal to zero point one six zero.

00:19:16.108 --> 00:19:21.080
Therefore ethane is zero point zero six zero zero mole and methane zero point

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.488
zero four zero zero mole.

00:19:23.128 --> 00:19:25.988
Their mole fractions are sixty and forty percent respectively.

00:19:26.948 --> 00:19:31.048
Water production is two a plus three b, or zero point two six zero mole.

00:19:32.028 --> 00:19:36.128
Oxygen demand is two a plus three point five b, or zero point two nine zero

00:19:36.128 --> 00:19:36.388
mole.

00:19:36.388 --> 00:19:41.128
With supplied molar masses sixteen and thirty, mean mixture molar mass is

00:19:41.128 --> 00:19:42.768
twenty four point four grams per mole.

00:19:43.468 --> 00:19:47.208
These results assume only the two named gases and complete formation of carbon

00:19:47.208 --> 00:19:48.208
dioxide and water.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:53.880
Parts per million is a ratio, so first identify whether the stated basis is

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:55.900
mass, volume or particle number.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:20:01.880
On a mass basis, one part per million means one milligram solute per kilogram

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:02.500
of solution.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:06.800
It is not automatically one milligram per litre.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:10.900
Consider a supplied solution with solute mass concentration three point zero

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:16.240
zero milligrams per litre and density one point two zero kilograms per litre.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:19.680
One litre therefore has solution mass one point two zero kilograms.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:24.958
Dividing three milligrams by that mass gives two point five zero milligrams

00:20:24.958 --> 00:20:25.120
per

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:28.860
kilogram, or two point five zero mass parts per million.

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:34.120
In a dilute aqueous solution whose density is approximately one kilogram per

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:38.105
litre, milligrams per litre can numerically approximate mass parts per

00:20:38.105 --> 00:20:38.560
million,

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:41.480
but the density assumption must be stated.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:47.040
Mass parts per billion use a factor of one billion instead of one million, so

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:50.680
one mass part per million equals one thousand mass parts per billion.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:55.740
The denominator is the total solution mass, not just the solvent mass.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:21:00.380
Keep the concentration basis attached to the number rather than using a trace

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:01.860
unit as a universal shortcut.

00:21:02.232 --> 00:21:06.292
Gas mixture composition can be expressed in several different ways.

00:21:07.332 --> 00:21:10.952
Suppose an ideal mixture contains two moles helium and one mole nitrogen

00:21:10.952 --> 00:21:12.372
molecules at a common temperature.

00:21:13.092 --> 00:21:17.092
The total amount is three moles, so the helium mole fraction is two thirds.

00:21:17.632 --> 00:21:21.552
If the total pressure is three bar, Dalton's law gives a helium partial

00:21:21.552 --> 00:21:24.872
pressure of two bar and nitrogen partial pressure of one bar.

00:21:25.532 --> 00:21:29.752
With supplied molar masses four and twenty eight grams per mole, the helium

00:21:29.752 --> 00:21:32.752
mass is eight grams and nitrogen mass twenty eight grams.

00:21:33.672 --> 00:21:37.052
Helium therefore contributes only eight of the thirty six grams, a mass

00:21:37.052 --> 00:21:39.272
fraction of two ninths rather than two thirds.

00:21:39.992 --> 00:21:44.312
The mean molar mass is total mass divided by total amount, or twelve grams per

00:21:44.312 --> 00:21:44.452
mole.

00:21:45.232 --> 00:21:49.332
In the ideal gas model, density equals pressure times mean molar mass divided

00:21:49.332 --> 00:21:53.392
by the gas constant and absolute temperature, using consistent units.

00:21:54.212 --> 00:21:58.272
This relation can connect density to composition, but cannot uniquely identify

00:21:58.272 --> 00:22:01.232
every possible mixture without knowing which gases are present.

00:22:02.352 --> 00:22:06.132
Mole fraction counts chemical entities, whereas mass fraction weights each

00:22:06.132 --> 00:22:07.232
entity by its molar mass.

00:22:07.488 --> 00:22:11.828
A precipitation calculation starts with the ions that actually react rather

00:22:11.828 --> 00:22:14.268
than every ion written in the molecular equation.

00:22:15.288 --> 00:22:19.768
Mix twenty five point zero zero millilitres of zero point one zero zero molar

00:22:19.768 --> 00:22:24.224
silver nitrate with forty point zero zero millilitres of zero point zero five

00:22:24.224 --> 00:22:26.068
zero zero molar sodium chloride.

00:22:26.708 --> 00:22:31.388
The initial silver ion amount is two point five zero millimoles and chloride

00:22:31.388 --> 00:22:33.328
amount two point zero zero millimoles.

00:22:33.328 --> 00:22:38.228
In the simplified complete precipitation model, the one to one net ionic

00:22:38.228 --> 00:22:42.388
equation makes chloride limiting, giving two point zero zero millimoles silver

00:22:42.388 --> 00:22:42.748
chloride.

00:22:43.608 --> 00:22:47.428
Using the supplied molar mass one hundred forty three point five grams per

00:22:47.428 --> 00:22:50.868
mole, the precipitate mass is zero point two eight seven gram.

00:22:51.668 --> 00:22:54.688
Excess silver ion is zero point five zero zero millimole.

00:22:55.368 --> 00:23:00.228
If final liquid volume is approximated by the sum, sixty five millilitres, its

00:23:00.228 --> 00:23:04.050
excess silver concentration is about zero point zero zero seven six nine

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:04.368
molar.

00:23:05.488 --> 00:23:09.388
Sodium and nitrate are spectator ions in this net reaction; they have not

00:23:09.388 --> 00:23:10.388
disappeared from solution.

00:23:11.348 --> 00:23:15.688
Finite solubility, complex formation and volume changes would require a more

00:23:15.688 --> 00:23:16.348
detailed model.

00:23:17.268 --> 00:23:20.888
State those approximations before treating the limiting ion result as exact

00:23:20.888 --> 00:23:21.868
experimental recovery.

00:23:22.656 --> 00:23:27.356
Atom economy is a supporting stoichiometric measure of how much reactant mass

00:23:27.356 --> 00:23:29.916
enters the desired product in a balanced net reaction.

00:23:30.676 --> 00:23:34.056
It differs from the percentage yield measured in a particular experiment.

00:23:34.956 --> 00:23:38.816
Consider ethanoic acid plus ethanol forming ethyl ethanoate and water.

00:23:39.716 --> 00:23:44.436
Use rounded molar masses sixty, forty six, eighty eight and eighteen grams per

00:23:44.436 --> 00:23:45.036
mole, respectively.

00:23:45.796 --> 00:23:49.405
One stoichiometric reaction unit starts with one hundred six grams of

00:23:49.405 --> 00:23:49.876
reactants

00:23:49.876 --> 00:23:53.816
and forms eighty eight grams of desired ester plus eighteen grams of water.

00:23:54.436 --> 00:23:58.743
The ester atom economy is therefore eighty eight divided by one hundred six,

00:23:58.743 --> 00:23:58.856
or

00:23:58.856 --> 00:24:00.876
about eighty three point zero percent.

00:24:00.876 --> 00:24:04.876
Even a hypothetical one hundred percent ester yield would not change that

00:24:04.876 --> 00:24:05.756
stoichiometric fraction.

00:24:06.816 --> 00:24:10.556
If an experiment starting with the stated stoichiometric amounts instead

00:24:10.556 --> 00:24:14.716
isolates ester at eighty percent of theoretical yield, its ester mass is

00:24:14.716 --> 00:24:15.896
seventy point four grams.

00:24:17.036 --> 00:24:21.796
Product purity is yet another question: an impure recovered sample mass cannot

00:24:21.796 --> 00:24:23.656
automatically be counted as pure ester.

00:24:24.816 --> 00:24:28.896
This simplified atom economy calculation excludes solvents and other process

00:24:28.896 --> 00:24:33.056
inputs and therefore does not measure every environmental impact or practical

00:24:33.056 --> 00:24:33.576
waste stream.

00:24:34.696 --> 00:24:38.696
Keep balanced reaction design, reaction yield and recovered sample purity as

00:24:38.696 --> 00:24:39.316
separate quantities.

00:24:39.672 --> 00:24:43.392
An average mass does not always determine every isotope abundance.

00:24:44.272 --> 00:24:48.232
Use a hypothetical element with only three isotopes, assigned integer masses

00:24:48.232 --> 00:24:52.803
twenty four, twenty five and twenty six, and supplied average twenty four

00:24:52.803 --> 00:24:53.116
point

00:24:53.116 --> 00:24:54.752
three zero atomic mass units.

00:24:55.432 --> 00:24:57.032
Their number fractions add to one.

00:24:57.572 --> 00:25:01.172
The weighted mass equation gives twenty four times the first fraction plus

00:25:01.172 --> 00:25:05.452
twenty five times the second plus twenty six times the third equal to twenty

00:25:05.452 --> 00:25:07.052
four point three zero.

00:25:07.052 --> 00:25:11.092
Subtract twenty four times the fraction sum to obtain second fraction plus

00:25:11.092 --> 00:25:14.432
twice the third equal to zero point three zero.

00:25:14.432 --> 00:25:18.052
That leaves one free choice, not a unique three isotope composition.

00:25:18.832 --> 00:25:23.552
For example, fractions zero point seven zero, zero point three zero and zero

00:25:23.552 --> 00:25:29.172
satisfy both equations, as do zero point eight five, zero and zero point one

00:25:29.172 --> 00:25:29.672
five.

00:25:29.672 --> 00:25:33.252
Now add an independent measurement that the mass twenty five isotope fraction

00:25:33.252 --> 00:25:35.052
is zero point one zero.

00:25:35.052 --> 00:25:39.012
The heavy fraction becomes zero point one zero and the light fraction zero

00:25:39.012 --> 00:25:40.292
point eight zero.

00:25:40.292 --> 00:25:42.852
All fractions must remain nonnegative.

00:25:42.852 --> 00:25:46.892
These are number fractions in a stated integer mass model, not isotope mass

00:25:46.892 --> 00:25:51.052
fractions or a claim that every real element has those exact isotopic masses.

00:25:51.528 --> 00:25:56.428
An equivalent amount is defined relative to a reaction, so it should not be

00:25:56.428 --> 00:25:58.428
treated as a new fixed molecular identity.

00:25:59.068 --> 00:26:04.068
In an acid base comparison, specify how many protons each acid molecule

00:26:04.068 --> 00:26:06.868
transfers at the stated stoichiometric endpoint.

00:26:08.348 --> 00:26:12.988
Phosphoric acid taken to dihydrogen phosphate consumes one hydroxide per acid

00:26:12.988 --> 00:26:13.408
molecule.

00:26:14.328 --> 00:26:19.368
Taken to hydrogen phosphate, it consumes two; taken formally to phosphate, it

00:26:19.368 --> 00:26:20.008
consumes three.

00:26:20.808 --> 00:26:25.505
A zero point one zero zero molar solution therefore corresponds to zero point

00:26:25.505 --> 00:26:29.568
one zero zero, zero point two zero zero or zero point three zero zero

00:26:29.568 --> 00:26:32.608
equivalents per litre for those respective definitions.

00:26:33.848 --> 00:26:37.808
These are often called normalities, but the underlying acid molarity has not

00:26:37.808 --> 00:26:40.968
changed merely because the selected reaction endpoint changed.

00:26:42.268 --> 00:26:45.468
Consider two hundred fifty millilitres of that acid taken to hydrogen

00:26:45.468 --> 00:26:45.928
phosphate.

00:26:46.618 --> 00:26:50.468
Acid amount is twenty five millimoles, requiring fifty millimoles hydroxide.

00:26:51.348 --> 00:26:55.738
A zero point two zero zero molar hydroxide solution supplies this in two

00:26:55.738 --> 00:26:57.688
hundred fifty millilitres.

00:26:57.688 --> 00:27:01.508
This is formal endpoint stoichiometry, not a claim that all three acid

00:27:01.508 --> 00:27:05.248
dissociations are equally strong or that every indicator detects every

00:27:05.248 --> 00:27:05.648
endpoint.

00:27:06.848 --> 00:27:09.128
Read the reaction before choosing an equivalence factor.

00:27:09.336 --> 00:27:13.796
Gas density can reveal a change in molecule count without any loss of mass.

00:27:14.776 --> 00:27:19.316
Consider an ideal gas initially containing only dinitrogen tetroxide, with

00:27:19.316 --> 00:27:21.536
supplied molar mass ninety two grams per mole.

00:27:22.436 --> 00:27:27.356
If fraction alpha dissociates into nitrogen dioxide, an initial amount n zero

00:27:27.356 --> 00:27:33.136
becomes n zero times one minus alpha of dinitrogen tetroxide and twice n zero

00:27:33.136 --> 00:27:34.716
alpha of nitrogen dioxide.

00:27:35.436 --> 00:27:40.146
Total gas amount is n zero times one plus alpha, so mean molar mass is ninety

00:27:40.146 --> 00:27:41.856
two divided by one plus alpha.

00:27:42.856 --> 00:27:46.196
At matched temperature and pressure, relative density compared with hydrogen

00:27:46.196 --> 00:27:51.156
gas is the molar mass ratio; use supplied hydrogen molar mass two.

00:27:51.156 --> 00:27:54.575
An observed relative density about thirty point six seven therefore

00:27:54.575 --> 00:27:55.136
corresponds

00:27:55.136 --> 00:27:59.456
to mean molar mass about sixty one point three three and dissociation fraction

00:27:59.456 --> 00:28:00.316
about one half.

00:28:00.776 --> 00:28:04.314
Starting with one hundred millimoles gives approximately fifty millimoles

00:28:04.314 --> 00:28:04.556
dimer

00:28:04.556 --> 00:28:08.816
and one hundred millimoles monomer, total one hundred fifty millimoles gas.

00:28:09.636 --> 00:28:12.796
The nitrogen dioxide mole fraction is two thirds, not one half.

00:28:13.336 --> 00:28:15.836
The original mass remains nine point two zero grams.

00:28:16.616 --> 00:28:20.530
Other gases, nonideal behaviour or changing comparison conditions would

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:20.916
require

00:28:20.916 --> 00:28:21.516
another model.

00:28:21.744 --> 00:28:26.184
A high conversion does not guarantee that every reacted molecule becomes the

00:28:26.184 --> 00:28:26.924
desired product.

00:28:27.724 --> 00:28:31.107
Use a supporting inventory model with two hypothetical one to one

00:28:31.107 --> 00:28:31.784
isomerization

00:28:31.784 --> 00:28:35.224
pathways: A forms desired B or undesired C.

00:28:36.144 --> 00:28:40.964
Feed one hundred millimoles A and observe twenty unreacted A, sixty B and

00:28:40.964 --> 00:28:41.484
twenty C.

00:28:42.124 --> 00:28:45.744
Conversion is reacted A divided by feed A: eighty percent.

00:28:45.744 --> 00:28:50.844
Define selectivity here as desired B divided by reacted A, giving sixty over

00:28:50.844 --> 00:28:52.924
eighty, or seventy five percent.

00:28:52.924 --> 00:28:57.544
Feed based desired yield is sixty over one hundred, or sixty percent.

00:28:57.544 --> 00:29:01.824
Under these stated one to one definitions, yield equals conversion times

00:29:01.824 --> 00:29:02.364
selectivity.

00:29:03.164 --> 00:29:07.568
Some contexts instead quote a desired to undesired product ratio, which here

00:29:07.568 --> 00:29:07.684
is

00:29:07.684 --> 00:29:11.784
three; always read the definition rather than assuming those numbers mean the

00:29:11.784 --> 00:29:12.284
same thing.

00:29:12.284 --> 00:29:16.524
If ninety percent of the unreacted twenty millimoles A is recovered for a new

00:29:16.524 --> 00:29:18.444
pass, eighteen can be recycled.

00:29:19.424 --> 00:29:22.777
Maintaining a one hundred millimole reactor feed then requires eighty two

00:29:22.777 --> 00:29:23.704
millimoles fresh A.

00:29:24.284 --> 00:29:28.144
Do not count the desired or undesired products as recovered unreacted reagent.

00:29:28.864 --> 00:29:33.084
Other reaction coefficients, recycle compositions or product recovery losses

00:29:33.084 --> 00:29:34.764
require expanded balances.

00:29:35.544 --> 00:29:39.944
A gas collected over water can contain both the intended gas and water vapor.

00:29:40.804 --> 00:29:44.536
In the ideal mixture comparison, total gas pressure is the sum of their

00:29:44.536 --> 00:29:44.904
partial

00:29:44.904 --> 00:29:45.304
pressures.

00:29:46.284 --> 00:29:50.704
Use the actual gas pressure supplied for the sample, not an atmospheric value

00:29:50.704 --> 00:29:53.344
that might require a separate liquid level correction.

00:29:54.024 --> 00:29:58.184
In a stipulated example at one common temperature, total pressure is one

00:29:58.184 --> 00:30:03.180
atmosphere, water vapor partial pressure is zero point zero three zero

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:07.564
atmosphere, gas volume is two point four five liters, and the supplied R T

00:30:07.564 --> 00:30:11.964
product is twenty four point five liter atmospheres per mole.

00:30:11.964 --> 00:30:16.264
Dry gas partial pressure is zero point nine seven zero atmosphere.

00:30:16.264 --> 00:30:20.233
Its amount is therefore zero point nine seven times two point four five

00:30:20.233 --> 00:30:20.624
divided

00:30:20.624 --> 00:30:25.624
by twenty four point five, or zero point zero nine seven zero mole.

00:30:25.624 --> 00:30:29.564
Using total pressure would instead count zero point one zero zero mole of the

00:30:29.564 --> 00:30:30.324
whole gas mixture.

00:30:31.164 --> 00:30:35.124
This correction assumes the collected gas does not react significantly with

00:30:35.124 --> 00:30:37.444
water and that the given vapor pressure applies.

00:30:38.284 --> 00:30:41.664
Identify the gas species being counted before using a molar volume shortcut.

00:30:42.696 --> 00:30:46.596
A gas mixture's average molar mass depends on which fractions are supplied.

00:30:47.456 --> 00:30:50.356
Equal masses are not equal mole amounts when molar masses differ.

00:30:51.176 --> 00:30:56.276
Use rounded atomic molar masses four for helium and twenty for neon, in grams

00:30:56.276 --> 00:30:56.656
per mole.

00:30:57.296 --> 00:31:01.945
A mixture containing ten grams of each has two point five moles helium and

00:31:01.945 --> 00:31:02.196
zero

00:31:02.196 --> 00:31:03.256
point five mole neon.

00:31:04.016 --> 00:31:08.916
Total amount is three moles and total mass twenty grams, so mean molar mass is

00:31:08.916 --> 00:31:12.476
twenty thirds, about six point six seven grams per mole.

00:31:13.196 --> 00:31:17.536
Helium mole fraction is five sixths and neon mole fraction one sixth.

00:31:18.496 --> 00:31:21.836
Weighting the two molar masses by those mole fractions gives the same result.

00:31:22.596 --> 00:31:26.716
For mass fractions instead, calculate the reciprocal of the sum of each mass

00:31:26.716 --> 00:31:29.176
fraction divided by its species molar mass.

00:31:30.216 --> 00:31:33.916
Simply averaging four and twenty would give twelve, which belongs to equal

00:31:33.916 --> 00:31:34.116
mole

00:31:34.116 --> 00:31:36.536
amounts rather than the equal mass mixture here.

00:31:37.296 --> 00:31:41.136
These noble gases are monatomic, so the counted gas entities are atoms.

00:31:42.196 --> 00:31:46.484
Ideal gas density can then use this mean molar mass at a specified pressure

00:31:46.484 --> 00:31:46.656
and

00:31:46.656 --> 00:31:46.876
temperature.

00:31:47.544 --> 00:31:51.968
A combustion calculation must not silently place all carbon into carbon

00:31:51.968 --> 00:31:52.404
dioxide

00:31:52.804 --> 00:31:55.144
if carbon monoxide is also a stated product.

00:31:56.264 --> 00:31:59.764
Consider a supplied material balance in which one mole of methane is consumed

00:31:59.764 --> 00:32:04.552
completely, producing zero point seven five mole carbon dioxide, zero point

00:32:04.552 --> 00:32:04.744
two

00:32:04.744 --> 00:32:08.908
five mole carbon monoxide and two moles water, with no other carbon or

00:32:08.908 --> 00:32:09.384
hydrogen

00:32:09.384 --> 00:32:09.844
products.

00:32:10.864 --> 00:32:14.544
Carbon is conserved because the two carbon containing products sum to one mole

00:32:14.544 --> 00:32:15.384
of carbon atoms.

00:32:16.144 --> 00:32:20.084
Hydrogen is conserved because two waters contain four moles hydrogen atoms.

00:32:21.224 --> 00:32:25.036
Oxygen atoms in the products total twice zero point seven five, plus zero

00:32:25.036 --> 00:32:25.297
point

00:32:25.297 --> 00:32:28.684
two five, plus two, giving three point seven five moles.

00:32:29.364 --> 00:32:33.273
The oxygen molecule requirement is half that amount, one point eight seven

00:32:33.273 --> 00:32:33.484
five

00:32:33.484 --> 00:32:33.784
moles.

00:32:34.724 --> 00:32:38.529
Using rounded molar masses gives sixteen grams methane plus sixty grams

00:32:38.529 --> 00:32:38.904
oxygen,

00:32:39.364 --> 00:32:43.764
matching thirty three grams carbon dioxide, seven grams carbon monoxide and

00:32:43.764 --> 00:32:44.844
thirty six grams water.

00:32:45.444 --> 00:32:49.384
Complete methane consumption is not the same as complete oxidation of every

00:32:49.384 --> 00:32:50.584
carbon to carbon dioxide.

00:32:51.524 --> 00:32:55.004
Use the supplied products and check each element before applying a familiar

00:32:55.004 --> 00:32:55.844
combustion ratio.

00:32:56.112 --> 00:33:00.472
A steady recycle balance differs from counting one isolated reaction pass.

00:33:01.352 --> 00:33:06.112
Use a supporting ideal process with one to one conversion of A into B, no side

00:33:06.112 --> 00:33:11.212
reaction, complete separation of B, perfect recycle of unreacted A and no

00:33:11.212 --> 00:33:11.552
purge.

00:33:12.252 --> 00:33:13.792
All rates are in moles per hour.

00:33:14.812 --> 00:33:18.812
Fresh A enters at one hundred, while the reactor converts forty percent of its

00:33:18.812 --> 00:33:20.192
own A feed each pass.

00:33:20.972 --> 00:33:23.072
Let total reactor A feed be F.

00:33:23.712 --> 00:33:28.292
The unconverted sixty percent returns, so the mixer balance is F equals one

00:33:28.292 --> 00:33:30.212
hundred plus zero point six F.

00:33:31.052 --> 00:33:35.472
Solving gives reactor feed two hundred fifty, recycle one hundred fifty and

00:33:35.472 --> 00:33:37.412
product B one hundred.

00:33:37.412 --> 00:33:41.572
Single pass conversion is forty percent, but overall fresh feed conversion is

00:33:41.572 --> 00:33:45.952
one hundred percent in this ideal steady state because no A leaves the process

00:33:45.952 --> 00:33:46.292
boundary.

00:33:47.112 --> 00:33:51.432
Recycle does not create new atoms; it counts the same unreacted material on

00:33:51.432 --> 00:33:52.612
repeated internal passages.

00:33:53.572 --> 00:33:57.252
Losses, purge, incomplete separation or side reactions change the overall

00:33:57.252 --> 00:33:57.672
balance.

00:33:58.752 --> 00:34:02.612
Distinguish the process boundary from the reactor boundary before assigning a

00:34:02.612 --> 00:34:03.092
conversion denominator.

00:34:03.624 --> 00:34:08.304
When two solutions of the same nonreacting solute are mixed, their solute

00:34:08.304 --> 00:34:10.764
amounts add, but their molarities do not.

00:34:11.704 --> 00:34:15.204
Convert every measured solution volume to litres before multiplying by

00:34:15.204 --> 00:34:15.524
molarity.

00:34:16.444 --> 00:34:20.444
Suppose one hundred millilitres of a one molar solution is combined with two

00:34:20.444 --> 00:34:22.884
hundred millilitres of a zero point five molar solution.

00:34:23.584 --> 00:34:26.804
Each portion contributes zero point one mole, so

00:34:26.804 --> 00:34:30.324
the final solute amount is zero point two mole.

00:34:30.324 --> 00:34:34.184
If the final measured solution volume is two hundred eighty five millilitres,

00:34:34.184 --> 00:34:39.004
the final molarity is zero point two divided by zero point two eight five,

00:34:39.004 --> 00:34:41.424
approximately zero point seven zero two molar.

00:34:42.304 --> 00:34:45.864
Dividing by three hundred millilitres would silently impose an additive volume

00:34:45.864 --> 00:34:48.164
assumption that contradicts the stated measurement.

00:34:48.984 --> 00:34:52.984
The volume change does not create or destroy solute particles in this selected

00:34:52.984 --> 00:34:53.284
model.

00:34:54.464 --> 00:34:58.264
Molarity uses the volume of the complete solution, not the amount of solvent

00:34:58.264 --> 00:34:58.784
poured in.

00:34:59.404 --> 00:35:03.624
If a chemical reaction, precipitation or evaporation occurs, its species

00:35:03.624 --> 00:35:05.284
balance must be included separately.

00:35:06.144 --> 00:35:10.165
The correct order is to conserve the named solute, establish the final

00:35:10.165 --> 00:35:10.624
solution

00:35:10.624 --> 00:35:13.284
volume, and only then calculate concentration.

00:35:14.472 --> 00:35:19.232
A withdrawal followed by replacement is not the same as adding solvent without

00:35:19.232 --> 00:35:20.352
removing any solute.

00:35:21.472 --> 00:35:24.992
Start with one hundred millilitres of a zero point four zero zero molar

00:35:24.992 --> 00:35:25.432
solution.

00:35:26.432 --> 00:35:29.452
It contains zero point zero four zero mole of solute.

00:35:30.492 --> 00:35:34.352
Remove a well mixed twenty five millilitre portion, then restore the total

00:35:34.352 --> 00:35:36.992
volume to one hundred millilitres using pure solvent.

00:35:37.772 --> 00:35:41.992
One quarter of the solute has been removed, leaving zero point zero three zero

00:35:41.992 --> 00:35:45.532
mole and a concentration of zero point three zero zero molar.

00:35:46.632 --> 00:35:50.032
Repeat the same withdrawal and replacement once more after mixing.

00:35:50.952 --> 00:35:55.452
This second portion contains only zero point zero zero seven five mole because

00:35:55.452 --> 00:35:57.052
the solution is already diluted.

00:35:57.932 --> 00:36:02.287
The remaining amount is zero point zero two two five mole, giving zero point

00:36:02.287 --> 00:36:04.612
two two five molar in the restored volume.

00:36:05.292 --> 00:36:08.912
The total removed amount is zero point zero one seven five mole.

00:36:10.052 --> 00:36:13.572
Multiplying the original concentration by the total withdrawn volume would

00:36:13.572 --> 00:36:14.792
overestimate that removal.

00:36:15.652 --> 00:36:19.512
The geometric factor is one minus the withdrawn fraction for each complete

00:36:19.512 --> 00:36:19.812
cycle.

00:36:20.632 --> 00:36:25.272
This assumes uniform mixing, identical restored volumes and no reaction or

00:36:25.272 --> 00:36:26.692
solute in the replacement solvent.

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:32.700
A combustion gas calculation must state both the oxygen excess and whether

00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:34.400
water remains in the measured gas.

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:39.980
Use a supplied ideal oxidizer containing twenty mole percent oxygen and eighty

00:36:39.980 --> 00:36:41.400
mole percent inert nitrogen.

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:46.520
This is a simplified mixture, not an exact composition of atmospheric air.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:51.680
Complete combustion of zero point one zero zero mole methane needs zero point

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:53.180
two zero zero mole oxygen.

00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:58.037
Twenty percent excess relative to that stoichiometric requirement supplies

00:36:58.037 --> 00:36:58.300
zero

00:36:58.300 --> 00:37:02.540
point two four zero mole oxygen and zero point nine six zero mole nitrogen.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:08.120
The ideal products contain zero point one zero zero mole carbon dioxide, zero

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:12.920
point two zero zero mole water, zero point zero four zero mole unused oxygen

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:14.540
and the unchanged nitrogen.

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:19.529
If all water is removed and the other gas amounts are unaffected, the dry

00:37:19.529 --> 00:37:19.820
total

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:21.720
is one point one zero zero mole.

00:37:22.490 --> 00:37:25.740
The dry carbon dioxide mole fraction is one eleventh.

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:30.780
If all product water instead remains vapour, the wet total is one point three

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:34.340
zero zero mole and the corresponding fraction is one thirteenth.

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:39.248
These are stipulated gas ledgers, excluding dissociation, incomplete

00:37:39.248 --> 00:37:39.820
combustion

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:41.340
and nitrogen reactions.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:45.880
Changing the denominator changes the reported composition without changing

00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:47.060
carbon dioxide production.

00:37:47.328 --> 00:37:51.808
A statement of purity must identify which material supplies the denominator.

00:37:52.928 --> 00:37:56.990
Consider a ten gram as received sample containing twenty percent water by

00:37:56.990 --> 00:37:57.268
mass.

00:37:57.988 --> 00:37:59.788
The dry solid mass is eight grams.

00:38:00.588 --> 00:38:04.488
If an independent assay says that seventy five percent of those dry solids is

00:38:04.488 --> 00:38:07.568
the active compound, its active mass is six grams.

00:38:08.348 --> 00:38:12.088
The wet sample therefore contains sixty percent active material by mass,

00:38:12.728 --> 00:38:16.068
although the dry solid assay remains seventy five percent.

00:38:16.068 --> 00:38:20.128
These percentages describe the same sample on different bases and do not

00:38:20.128 --> 00:38:21.188
contradict one another.

00:38:21.868 --> 00:38:26.148
If the active compound has a supplied molar mass of fifty grams per mole, the

00:38:26.148 --> 00:38:30.128
original wet sample contains zero point one two zero mole of that compound.

00:38:31.248 --> 00:38:34.548
Subtracting twenty percentage points directly from seventy five percent would

00:38:34.548 --> 00:38:36.928
mix denominators and give the wrong active mass.

00:38:37.468 --> 00:38:42.028
This calculation assumes water is a separately measured component and that

00:38:42.028 --> 00:38:46.068
drying would not decompose, volatilize or chemically transform the active

00:38:46.068 --> 00:38:46.468
compound.

00:38:47.528 --> 00:38:50.988
Water of crystallization may be part of a compound's formula rather than

00:38:50.988 --> 00:38:54.948
removable contamination, so that case needs a different species definition.

00:38:55.988 --> 00:38:59.308
Write the mass tree before converting the named active material into moles.

00:38:59.928 --> 00:39:04.008
A mass plateau during dehydration need not represent the anhydrous salt.

00:39:04.968 --> 00:39:08.788
Consider a supplied hypothetical hydrate of a salt whose anhydrous molar mass

00:39:08.788 --> 00:39:10.528
is one hundred sixty grams per mole.

00:39:11.308 --> 00:39:15.688
A ten gram sample first reaches a mass of eight point five six grams and

00:39:15.688 --> 00:39:17.748
finally six point four zero grams.

00:39:18.828 --> 00:39:22.968
Assume the final material is independently identified as anhydrous salt, only

00:39:22.968 --> 00:39:26.868
water is lost, no salt is lost and no other decomposition occurs.

00:39:27.348 --> 00:39:32.168
The salt amount is six point four zero divided by one hundred sixty, or zero

00:39:32.168 --> 00:39:33.728
point zero four zero zero mole.

00:39:34.928 --> 00:39:38.748
The total water lost from the initial sample is three point six zero grams,

00:39:39.368 --> 00:39:41.508
corresponding to zero point two zero zero mole.

00:39:42.628 --> 00:39:45.928
Dividing water amount by salt amount gives five waters per formula unit

00:39:45.928 --> 00:39:46.348
initially.

00:39:47.308 --> 00:39:51.365
The first mass loss is one point four four grams, or zero point zero eight

00:39:51.365 --> 00:39:51.584
zero

00:39:51.584 --> 00:39:55.528
zero mole of water, meaning two waters per salt unit were removed at that

00:39:55.528 --> 00:39:55.768
stage.

00:39:56.398 --> 00:39:59.568
The intermediate therefore retains three waters per formula unit.

00:40:00.328 --> 00:40:03.368
A two water loss does not mean the starting solid was a dihydrate.

00:40:04.208 --> 00:40:07.588
These mass data alone do not establish the structures or purity of every

00:40:07.588 --> 00:40:11.348
plateau; the hydrate assignments depend on the stated phase and loss

00:40:11.348 --> 00:40:11.788
assumptions.

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:16.220
In consecutive reactions, an intermediate can be formed in one step and

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:17.160
consumed in the next.

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:23.200
Use a stipulated one to one sequence A to B followed by B to C, with no side

00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:27.040
reactions and one conserved molecular unit in every species.

00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:30.760
Start with one mole of A and no B or C.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:34.960
Suppose the first reaction extent is zero point eight zero mole and the second

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:36.500
is zero point three zero mole.

00:40:37.060 --> 00:40:41.420
The first step consumes zero point eight zero mole of A and forms that amount

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:41.740
of B.

00:40:42.340 --> 00:40:45.960
The second consumes zero point three zero mole of B and forms C.

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:50.800
Final amounts are therefore zero point two zero mole A, zero point five zero

00:40:50.800 --> 00:40:53.440
mole B and zero point three zero mole C.

00:40:53.960 --> 00:40:56.980
Their sum is one mole in this particular one to one model.

00:40:57.920 --> 00:41:02.185
Conversion of A is eighty percent, but the final B yield relative to starting

00:41:02.185 --> 00:41:02.240
A

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:03.700
is fifty percent.

00:41:03.700 --> 00:41:08.640
The final B selectivity relative to converted A is zero point five divided by

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:11.840
zero point eight, or sixty two point five percent.

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:15.920
The second extent is an absolute molar amount, not a thirty percent yield

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:18.260
factor to multiply blindly by the first extent.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:22.740
More complicated stoichiometries require their own coefficient balances.

00:41:23.160 --> 00:41:26.960
A limiting reactant calculation can depend on whether the measured ranges

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:30.120
overlap after division by stoichiometric coefficients.

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:36.840
Consider the model reaction one A plus two B giving one P, driven to complete

00:41:36.840 --> 00:41:39.780
consumption of the limiting reactant with no side reactions.

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:45.380
In a supplied bounded dataset, A lies between zero point nine eight and one

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:50.168
point zero two mole, while B lies between two point zero nine and two point

00:41:50.168 --> 00:41:50.360
one

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:51.000
one mole.

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:56.440
The available reaction extent from B is its amount divided by two, between one

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:59.960
point zero four five and one point zero five five mole.

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:05.709
Even the largest allowed A amount is below the smallest B based extent, so A

00:42:05.709 --> 00:42:05.860
is

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:07.400
limiting for every permitted combination.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:13.080
The possible P amount spans zero point nine eight to one point zero two mole.

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:17.643
Now change the ranges to A between zero point nine five and one point zero

00:42:17.643 --> 00:42:17.890
five

00:42:17.890 --> 00:42:22.860
mole, and B between one point nine five and two point zero five mole.

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:26.780
The scaled ranges overlap, so either reactant can be limiting for allowed

00:42:26.780 --> 00:42:27.260
values.

00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:31.880
These are stipulated hard bounds, not standard deviations or confidence

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:32.280
intervals.

00:42:33.340 --> 00:42:37.280
Keeping more calculator digits does not resolve a physical ambiguity that the

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:38.140
measurements leave open.

00:42:38.328 --> 00:42:42.928
A hydrated crystal crop contains water, so its total mass is not the recovered

00:42:42.928 --> 00:42:44.528
mass of anhydrous solute.

00:42:45.328 --> 00:42:48.828
Start with a supplied hundred gram solution containing twenty grams of a

00:42:48.828 --> 00:42:52.368
hypothetical dissolved substance S and eighty grams of water.

00:42:52.968 --> 00:42:57.218
Its anhydrous molar mass is one hundred grams per mole, and the stipulated

00:42:57.218 --> 00:42:57.448
pure

00:42:57.448 --> 00:43:02.608
crystal phase is S with two waters per formula unit, with molar mass one

00:43:02.608 --> 00:43:04.048
hundred thirty six.

00:43:04.048 --> 00:43:07.888
Suppose thirteen point six grams of this pure hydrate separates, with no

00:43:07.888 --> 00:43:10.888
evaporation, entrained mother liquor or other loss.

00:43:11.648 --> 00:43:16.508
The crystals contain zero point one zero zero mole of S, so ten grams of

00:43:16.508 --> 00:43:20.788
anhydrous solute and three point six grams of water leave the liquid together.

00:43:21.628 --> 00:43:25.908
The remaining mother liquor contains ten grams of S and seventy six point four

00:43:25.908 --> 00:43:29.308
grams of water, for total mass eighty six point four grams.

00:43:30.188 --> 00:43:34.848
Its solute mass fraction is ten divided by eighty six point four, about eleven

00:43:34.848 --> 00:43:36.588
point five seven percent.

00:43:36.588 --> 00:43:42.228
The recovered solute fraction is ten divided by the original twenty, or fifty

00:43:42.228 --> 00:43:42.468
percent.

00:43:42.468 --> 00:43:46.708
Using the full crystal mass over initial solute mass would incorrectly count

00:43:46.708 --> 00:43:48.708
crystallization water as recovered solute.

00:43:49.268 --> 00:43:53.388
These balances use a supplied final crystal crop; they do not independently

00:43:53.388 --> 00:43:56.508
predict solubility or crystallization equilibrium.
